2025-12-14 13:29:01,384 - INFO - Generated response: Diabetic retinopathy is a major eye-related disorder that can occur in patients with diabetes. It refers to a group of complications caused by chronic and end-stage kidney disease, including severe macular edema (ME), glaucoma, central serous chorioretinopathy (CSCR), and optic atrophy (OA). ME is characterized by swelling of the retina that can cause visual loss. CSCR is a type of glaucoma caused by increased production of extracellular fluid in the vitreous. OA is a progressive degenerative eye disorder caused by progressive damage to the optic nerve, which results in reduced vision and sensitivity to light. There are several other complications associated with diabetic retinopathy, including cataracts, vasomotor symptoms (e.g., dry eyes), and postural hypotension.
