2025-12-14 13:30:33,919 - INFO - Generated response: The main symptoms of diabetic retinopathy are:
1. Chronic persistent retinal detachment (CPRD) - a new, small-sized hole or crack in the retina
2. Diabetic maculopapular syndrome (DMPS) - inflammatory skin disease with red, inflamed areas around eyes and on face
3. Retinopathy of prematurity (ROP) - a type of vision loss that occurs in babies born prematurely
4. Macular oedema (MO) - swelling or fluid buildup in the centre of the eye, leading to blurred vision and/or severe sight loss
5. Retinal detachment - a new, small-sized hole or crack in the retina
6. Diabetic optic neuropathy (DON) - impaired function of nerves in the eyes that affects visual perception, particularly night vision 
7. Retinopathy of prematurity (ROP) is also associated with microalbumineuria, which can occur as a result of prolonged and poorly managed diabetes
