2025-12-14 13:31:53,628 - INFO - Generated response: The major diagnosis for diabetic retinopathy involves an assessment and evaluation of the patient's visual, ocular, and neurological systems. Other diagnostic tools used include random plasmaglucose levels (OGTT), fasting plasma glucose levels (OGTT), and an oral glucose tolerance test (OGTT). HbA1c (glycated haemoglobin) is also assessed as a reference point for the diagnosis of T2DM. The diagnosis of diabetic retinopathy is made based on the presence of persistent damage to the retina, visual impairments, kidney-related complications, and neurological sequelae. These are discussed further in more detail below.
